import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Media generation"
  eyebrow="Capabilities"
  description="Provider-neutral image generation and media artifacts."
  prev={{ href: "/docs/knowledge/", title: "Project knowledge" }}
  next={{ href: "/docs/speech/", title: "Speech" }}
>
  <p>
    Roder generates images through a provider-neutral media API. Tool calls,
    app-server clients, SDKs, and the TUI all use the same request shape, and every
    output is persisted as a Roder-owned <code>MediaArtifact</code> before it is shown or
    attached to another turn.
  </p>

  <h2>Image providers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>openai</code> defaults to <code>gpt-image-2.5-flare</code> for everyday generation and editing. Select <code>gpt-image-2.5-sunburst</code> for precision work; older GPT Image model ids remain explicitly selectable.</li>
    <li><code>google</code> supports Gemini Nano Banana image models, including aspect ratio and image-size options where available.</li>
    <li><code>fake</code> is deterministic and always available for tests and offline profiles.</li>
  </ul>

  <pre><code>{`[media]
artifacts_dir = "~/.roder/artifacts"

[media.image_generation]
default_provider = "openai"
default_model = "gpt-image-2.5-flare"
max_outputs = 4
max_input_images = 16`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Set <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code> for OpenAI image generation. This uses separate API
    billing; Codex subscription sign-in does not authenticate this media provider or
    verify selection of a specific GPT Image 2.5 model. Without configuration, the
    default provider remains the deterministic offline <code>fake</code> provider.
    Image models appear in the media catalog, separate from chat models.
  </p>

  <h2>Generate and edit</h2>
  <p>
    GPT Image 2.5 accepts <code>quality</code> values <code>auto</code>, <code>low</code>,
    <code>medium</code>, <code>high</code>, <code>xhigh</code> and <code>max</code>.
    To edit an existing image through the tool or app server, pass its Roder artifact id
    in <code>inputArtifacts</code> and set <code>action</code> to <code>edit</code>.
    The default <code>auto</code> action also selects editing when input images are present.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`{
  "provider": "openai",
  "model": "gpt-image-2.5-sunburst",
  "prompt": "Simplify the background while keeping the subject",
  "action": "edit",
  "inputArtifacts": ["media-image-123"],
  "quality": "high",
  "outputFormat": "png"
}`}</code></pre>

  <h2>CLI and app server</h2>
  <pre><code>roder media providers --json
roder media models --provider google
roder media generate "a clean terminal product hero" --provider openai --model gpt-image-2.5-flare</code></pre>

  <p>
    App-server clients call <code>media/image/providers/list</code> and
    <code>media/image/generate</code>. Generated artifacts then flow through the existing
    <code>media/list</code>, <code>media/read</code>, <code>media/thumbnail</code>,
    <code>media/delete</code>, and <code>media/attachToTurn</code> methods.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
